# Copy to `.env.local`. That file is git-ignored; this one is committed as the contract.

# ── Public (NEXT_PUBLIC_*) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Anything prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ is INLINED INTO THE BROWSER BUNDLE at build time.
# It is readable by anyone who opens devtools. Never put a secret behind this prefix —
# the prefix is not a security boundary, it is the opposite: an explicit declaration
# that the value is public.
# NestJS runs on 3001; this Next.js app runs on 3000.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001/api

# ── Server-only ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, so these stay on the server and never reach the bundle.

# Encrypts and signs NextAuth's session cookie (a JWE — so unlike an ordinary JWT the contents
# are NOT readable by the user). Also protects NextAuth's own CSRF token.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# NOT the same as the backend's JWT_ACCESS_SECRET. Two secrets, two systems:
#   AUTH_SECRET       → encrypts the NextAuth session cookie   (this app)
#   JWT_ACCESS_SECRET → signs the access token NestJS issues    (the API)
#
# Keeping them separate is real defence in depth: if AUTH_SECRET leaks, an attacker can forge a
# convincing session cookie — but it contains no VALID API access token, so every real request
# still 401s. They would be "logged in" to a UI that can't fetch anything.
#
# If it changes while users are signed in, every session cookie becomes undecryptable and everyone
# is silently logged out. Availability problem, not a security one — but rotate deliberately.
#
# v5 reads AUTH_SECRET; v4 called it NEXTAUTH_SECRET. Both names appear in tutorials.
AUTH_SECRET=

# The canonical origin of this app. v5 can usually infer it, but setting it explicitly avoids
# callback-URL surprises behind a proxy. Note this is the FRONTEND's port (3000), not the API's.
NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
